description:
## Why This Task Exists

Portable ciphertext alone cannot recover role-specific identities, local checkpoints, or witness-service safety state. Separating those custody domains and proving real restore drills prevents a backup file from being presented as recovery for authority or availability it does not contain.

## Outcome

Owners can create, verify, drill, and restore bounded encrypted backups for portable vault state, vault-principal identities, approver identities, and local audit/checkpoint state without mixing custody domains. J23 separately owns replay-safe witness-service backup and restoration.

## Required Work

- Define authenticated backup manifests and explicit roots for portable vault, role-specific identities, and principal-local verification state.
- Report exactly which direct items, witnessed client/approver roles, and local checkpoints a backup can recover; never imply that it recovers witness availability, quorum, or juryd state.
- Restore portable .jury/vault.json only into an absent target and keep identities and audit/checkpoint material outside the worktree.
- Revalidate witnessed policy, configured topology, quorum satisfiability, retained checkpoint ancestry, and required external witness recovery before private use.
- Support passphrase resealing and same-role identity replacement without pretending revoked actors lose retained ciphertext or that client backup can replace witness-service recovery.
- Make cross-root publication failures explicit and recoverable.
- Never execute Git or trust paths embedded in a backup.
- Backup create, verify, drill, and restore must never add a direct slot, rewrite an item's access mode, or change whether its quorum claim is valid; witnessed-only ciphertext stays closed until topology, checkpoint, approver, witness, and J23 recovery requirements pass.

## Required Tests

Cover all three identity roles; wrong credentials; corruption/truncation; missing components; path traversal/symlinks; each crash boundary; repository-local and detached restores; old-state retention; unavailable/restored/rotated witnesses; impossible post-restore quorum; secret-leak scans; and explicit assertions that every backup lifecycle leaves direct-slot sets and computed quorum status unchanged.

## Acceptance Criteria

- backup is clearly more sensitive than transfer;
- backup status states exactly which direct items, role-specific identities, and local verification state it can recover;
- backup create, verify, drill, and restore never add a direct slot, rewrite an
  item's access mode, or change whether its quorum claim is valid;
- client or owner backup never claims to recover juryd, witness replay state, quorum availability, or external anchors;
- restore never overwrites an existing identity or vault;
- recovery readiness distinguishes creation, verification, a real drill, and outstanding external witness recovery;
- witnessed private use remains blocked when restored policy/topology/checkpoint state is stale or quorum is unavailable;
- repository-local restore never writes identity or rollback evidence into Git and performs no implicit Git operation.

## Dependencies

J23 separately owns witness-service recovery; J18 and J26 depend directly on J23 for that boundary. Dependencies: J04, J07, J09, and J10. Unblocks J18, J24, and J25.

## Legacy Baseline and Provenance

Use https://github.com/bpcakes/jig-sh.git at exact commit eed70cee337b0067ed92deb9fa05017b0b284605; a sibling ../jig-sh is only an object source after the commit and every selected path verifies. If the exact object or a named path is unavailable, this task blocks rather than substituting another revision, build output, or the preserved source-plan snapshot.

