## Active Witnessed 0.x Scope

This outcome is mandatory for the first experimental witnessed-access release. It remains pre-alpha, must not be used for real secrets, and may claim only the exact properties proved under the accepted J19 construction and deployment assumptions.

## Project Boundary

Jury is intended as a standalone CLI/TUI plus self-hostable witness service. Native storage is jury-vault format v1. Jury must not depend on Jig at runtime, store jig:// routing in cryptographic state, expose private keys or epoch roots to application adapters, or use non-generic fixtures. The repository remains pre-alpha and must not claim that it protects real secrets.

## Why This Task Exists

Witnessed UX must bind meaningful approval to the exact revision seal and call the same narrow item-access boundary without leaking contribution material.

## Required Design Contract

The Outcome, Scope, Required Tests, and Acceptance Criteria below are normative. docs/architecture.md and docs/jury-v1-master-plan.md define the shared gate and protocol context; no implementation may substitute a weaker construction.

## Security Hardening Amendment

The endpoint evaluates quorum for one content role, revision, and RevisionSealId, then passes only ProtectedRevisionSecrets through ItemAccessProvider.


## Outcome

Users and machines can create, inspect, approve/deny, execute, cancel, and
observe witnessed requests through stable CLI contracts.

## Scope

- implement request construction and client signatures;
- construct the canonical action manifest and bind its digest into every
  approval-capable request;
- verify its policy-authenticated `ApprovalTargetV1`; human approval requires an
  entitled private name or owner-signed non-secret review labels for every item,
  field, working directory, and output destination, while opaque selectors
  remain limited to explicitly typed automatic policy;
- run the common request/action-manifest consistency validator before rendering
  or signing any approval;
- implement a separately protected approver identity and strict signed
  `ApprovalDecisionV1`; never reuse a vault-principal or witness key implicitly;
- generate protected request-specific session keys;
- contact configured witnesses and validate responses;
- implement `WitnessedItemAccessProvider`, evaluate quorum, and call the common `ItemAccessProvider` revision-secret boundary without exposing contribution material;
- connect J10 pending/approved/denied/expired states to real J20/J21 responses with no secret-bearing session snapshot;
- expose human and JSON request/approval/status commands with complete verified
  manifest rendering and no digest-only approval path;
- make witnessed read/inject/exec the governed default while retaining explicit direct mode with visible unilateral status;
- make expiry, denial, stale, replay, unavailable, and insufficient quorum
  distinct.

## Required Tests

- foreground automatic and asynchronous approval workflows;
- forged/replayed/revoked approval identities and wrong request, manifest,
  policy, witness set, key epoch, or expiry;
- manifest absence, one-bit changes, typed secret placeholders, lossy rendering,
  truncation at every supported terminal width, opaque targets, forged/stale
  review labels, opaque field/directory/sink commitments, and missing name
  entitlement;
- validly signed request/manifest pairs whose duplicated fields disagree;
- cancellation races;
- malicious response and wrong-session attacks;
- command digest changes;
- no child spawn before full authorization;
- no contribution in output, state snapshots, or logs;
- end-to-end witnessed-only fixture: create item, request exact revision, collect independent approvals and contributions, read/inject/exec, verify receipt, then reject the next revision until a fresh quorum authorizes its new seal.

## Acceptance Criteria

- direct and witnessed modes share use-case APIs;
- request broadening always changes the digest;
- interactive approval signs only after complete verified action-manifest
  rendering with policy-authenticated meaningful target, field, working-
  directory, and output-destination displays; an opaque digest, target, or
  commitment alone can never be approved;
- every counted approval is a current independent approver signature, not a
  transport-authenticated action;
- receipt nonclaims appear in user documentation.
- a witnessed-only item completes read, inject, and exec without a direct slot, raw key, epoch root, or reusable contribution;
- the next revision seal remains inaccessible until a fresh authorized quorum completes.

## Plan Dependencies

J10, J13, J14, J19, J21, J23.

## Unblocks

J24, J25, J26.


## Live Tracker Dependencies

- J10: `jury-qv4.2.9`
- J13: `jury-qv4.3.2`
- J14: `jury-qv4.3.3`
- J19: `jury-qv4.4.1`
- J21: `jury-qv4.4.3`
- J23: `jury-qv4.4.5`

## Legacy Baseline and Provenance

No J22-specific Jig v2 source is authorized for extraction. Implement witnessed open, approval, and execution UX from the complete J10/J13/J14/J19/J21/J23 contracts in this Bead. The pinned Jig repository may be consulted only as non-normative historical context; do not import its whole-vault UX assumptions and do not add a Jury runtime dependency on Jig.

## Jig v3 Retirement Provenance

Carries Jury-only witnessed UX work and the Jury disposition for relevant Jig v3 CLI outcomes. The exact unshipped source design remains in docs/provenance/jig-vault-security-scopes-plan.source.md.

